2026-09-29T23:50Z ROOT CAUSE IDENTIFIED (was: fontconfig cache corruption, source unknown).
  METHOD: sandboxed reproduction with isolated HOME/XDG_CACHE_HOME in /tmp (real cache untouched).
    TEST C: host `fc-cache -rf` alone -> 38 real -le64.cache-9 files, no symlinks, 0 invalid.
    TEST D: google-chrome 154.0.8037.57 headless alone -> 38 real -le64.cache-12 PLUS 38 symlinks
            each of -cache-9, -cache-10, -cache-11 pointing at the v12 file.
  MECHANISM: Chrome writes font cache format v12 and back-links older names (9/10/11) to it.
  Host fontconfig 2.17.1 understands v9 only, so it opens -cache-9, follows the symlink into v12
  data, logs "invalid cache file: <hash>-le64.cache-9" and plasmashell SIGSEGVs in
  FcCharSetHasChar/QFontEngineMultiFontConfig -> no panel + black lock screen.
  The 4 hashes reported in the sandbox (a698622a, c57959a1, da43223d, bf3b770c) are IDENTICAL to
  the hashes in the 16:25 crash, confirming the same mechanism.
  TRIGGER: any Chrome launch. The 15:12/15:19 corrupt writes coincide with my own headless-Chrome
  Playwright probes for the Mastodon two-tab login test, which is why the 13:38 fc-cache repair
  appeared to "not hold".
  RULED OUT: WebODM containers (python3.9 use bundled libfontconfig.so.1.12.0 but HOME=/root inside
  the container, so they write their own cache); pCloud AppImage (380MB payload, no bundled
  fontconfig); podman-desktop (no bundled fontconfig); package corruption (dpkg -V plasma-workspace
  clean; libfontconfig1 2.17.1-3ubuntu1 intact, no apt transaction for fontconfig today).
  NOTE: `ldd /opt/google/chrome/chrome` resolves to the system libfontconfig.so.1, which does not
  explain a v12 writer; recorded as an open discrepancy - the empirical isolation above stands.
  OUTSTANDING (needs operator approval): isolate Chrome's font cache via a wrapper with its own
  XDG_CACHE_HOME, or accept fc-cache -r as a recurring band-aid.
2026-09-30T00:00Z FIX APPLIED (operator-requested: stop Chrome writing the shared fontconfig cache).
  Approach: fontconfig derives its cache dir from $XDG_CACHE_HOME and exposes no dedicated
  override, so Chrome is given its own cache home via a wrapper.
  Created (all new files, fully reversible by deletion):
    /home/scottw/.local/bin/google-chrome           (execs /opt/google/chrome/google-chrome)
    /home/scottw/.local/bin/google-chrome-stable    (same; covers PATH-based and scripted use)
    /home/scottw/.local/share/applications/google-chrome.desktop        (user override)
    /home/scottw/.local/share/applications/com.google.Chrome.desktop   (user override)
  Both system entries used Exec=/usr/bin/google-chrome-stable (absolute, so a PATH shim alone
  would not have intercepted them); user overrides in ~/.local/share/applications take precedence.
  ~/.local/bin is first on PATH, so CLI/scripted `google-chrome` also resolves to the wrapper.
  XDG_CONFIG_HOME intentionally untouched: browser profile stays in ~/.config/google-chrome.
  VERIFIED: the exact headless invocation that previously produced 38x v12 + 9/10/11 back-link
  symlinks now leaves the shared cache at 38x pure cache-9 with 0 "invalid cache file" warnings;
  the v12 set lands in /home/scottw/.cache/chrome/fontconfig instead.
  Desktop unaffected: plasmashell PID 28060 up 20m+, cache clean.
  REMAINING GAP: any future tool that invokes /usr/bin/google-chrome* by absolute path (bypassing
  PATH and the desktop entries) can still poison the shared cache; `fc-cache -r` repairs it.
